data.content:
## Migration Analysis

Your code has **three** Thread.Abort patterns:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` + `Stop()` calls `Abort()` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **ThreadAbortException as control flow** | catch block rolls back transaction | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort to continue** | Prevents rethrow so loop keeps running | Per-item `CancellationTokenSource` linked to parent |

### Why it breaks on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — the runtime **cannot** forcibly terminate a thread. `Thread.ResetAbort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are also gone. Your `Stop()` method will crash, and if `GetNextItem()` blocks, the thread will never exit.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Per-item CTS linked to the parent — replaces Thread.ResetAbort.
            // Cancelling the parent cancels this too, but each iteration
            // gets a fresh token so one cancelled item doesn't kill the loop.
            using var itemCts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(token);
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item, itemCts.Token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Only the per-item token was cancelled (e.g., timeout on one item).
                // Replaces the old ThreadAbortException + ResetAbort pattern.
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // Loop continues to the next item.
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // The parent token was cancelled — Stop() was called.
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();

        // Give the thread time to finish its current item.
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive, the thread is background — it will die at process exit.
        // There is NO way to forcibly kill it in modern .NET.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key changes explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative, not preemptive. The thread must check the token voluntarily.

2. **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — use the `when` filter to distinguish "cancel this item" vs "shut down the whole loop."

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → linked `CancellationTokenSource`** — each loop iteration creates a fresh child token. Cancelling one item doesn't tear down the thread.

4. **`GetNextItem()` must accept a token** — if it blocks (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take`), pass the token so it throws `OperationCanceledException` on shutdown instead of blocking forever.

5. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(TimeSpan)`** — `Join` actually waits for the thread to finish, rather than blindly sleeping.

---

### Critical pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| **`GetNextItem()` ignores the token** | `Stop()` will hang — the thread stays blocked and never checks cancellation. Pass the token to every blocking call. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and callbacks. Always `using` or `Dispose()`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | The `when (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter is essential — without it you can't tell a per-item cancel from a full shutdown. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. If `ProcessItem` runs for 30 seconds without checking, cancellation waits 30 seconds. Add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside long work. |
